From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 22:38:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: packet loss telemetry from the client's FEC
 reports

Moonlight tells a Sunshine host about every FEC block that lost packets:
clients that announce ML_FF_FEC_STATUS send SS_FRAME_FEC_STATUS (control
type 0x5502) when they recover a block with parity and when they give up
on one. Upstream drops the message as an unknown type, so the host only
learns about losses FEC could not hide (reference frame invalidation),
which is too late and too coarse to tune FEC or the bitrate by.

- The control thread decodes the report (big-endian fields at fixed
  offsets; a short payload or an empty block is ignored) and counts, per
  stream: packets lost (a recovered block: the gaps below the highest
  packet received; a block given up on: every packet that did not come),
  blocks recovered, blocks lost, and the worst block of the interval as
  lost packets per data shard (the FEC share that block would have
  needed). Relaxed atomics only; the control thread never takes a lock.
- The video broadcast thread also counts the packets (data and parity
  shards) it sends.
- Heartbeats gain, per stream and pooled in `metrics`: `packet_loss_pct`
  (lost per sent packet, 0.01 %), `burst_loss_pct` (worst block, 0.1 %),
  `fec_recovered`, `fec_failed`; `metrics.fec_percent` is the FEC share
  the next frame uses. The stream object (sessions and client events)
  gains `fec_feedback`: whether the client sends the reports at all.

The client sends at most 8 reports per 100 ms, so very lossy links are
under-counted; frame loss (`loss_pct`) stays as it was.

